6. State → Embedding Linear Regression

A more direct state-removal method was subsequently used.

Train:

Random frame split in this particular experiment

80% train / 20% test

State centered using training mean

Linear least-squares regression:

state → 512-D embedding

The learned matrix had shape:

[8, 512]

The state-predicted embedding component was:

Z_state = (S - mean(S)) W

Residual embedding:

Z_residual = Z_centered - Z_state

7. State → Embedding Explained Variance

For a 10,000-frame state → embedding regression experiment:

Per-embedding-dimension R²
Mean R²: 0.2828
Standard deviation: 10.34 due to many near-zero-variance dimensions / unstable per-dimension R²
Maximum R²: 0.6024 on held-out data
Minimum R²: approximately -126

The negative R² values are mainly a consequence of evaluating individual embedding dimensions with very small total variance; they should NOT be interpreted as meaningful negative information.

More useful aggregate result:

Residual variance ratio

After predicting embedding from state:

Residual variance / original variance = 0.6857

So approximately:

31.4% of average embedding variance is removed/explained by the linear state model.
68.6% remains after removing the state-predictable component.

Important caveat:

This is variance explained by a linear state→embedding model, not mutual information and not “31.4% of semantic information.”

